speechtext:
On the order:	Government notices of
motion:
June 9-The Prime Minister-The following proposed motion:-
That a special committee be appointed to consider and recommend changes that may be desirable in the War Measures Act;
That the committee consist of fifteen members to be designated by the house;
That the committee be empowered to sit during the sittings of the house;
That the committee have power to send for persons and papers;
That the committee have power to print such papers and evidence from day to day as it may deem advisable and that standing order 66 be suspended in relation to the printing thereof; and
That the committee be empowered to report from time to time.

Mr. Chairman, as I said earlier the United States does not have legislation parallel to this, because as far as lake Michigan is concerned their legislation covers exemption from pilotage and it is not an exemption from pilotage dues. That is the difference. As far as Canadian ships are concerned, they were exempted from compulsory pilotage on lake Michigan by legislation. As I said, it is not exactly the same thing because their legislation is not parallel with that in Canada. On the St. Lawrence we have compulsory pilotage dues, and as far as lake Michigan is concerned they talk about compulsory pilotage, period.
My hon. friend has said the pilots did not have a chance to express their views and make representations. I do not agree with that at all. We have met with the pilots many times; we have received briefs from them;

they have appeared before the committee, and I can assure my hon. friend and the committee that they had every opportunity to express their views and give us their opinion of this legislation. As I said earlier, not only were they advised that this amendment would be forthcoming when the bill was introduced in the house, but it was explained to them very clearly in November, 1960 that we intended to do this. That is when we requested the voluntary exemption of the United States ships and that is what they have been doing since the end of navigation last year.

I should like to supplement the remarks made by the hon. member for Vancouver East. I am concerned particularly with the treatment centres, and the provisions to which the hon. member for Vancouver East has already alluded, particularly as they relate to the narcotic addiction foundation of British Columbia. At the moment this is the only community based centre offering treatment and rehabilitation services for narcotic addicts, including those who are discharged from federal or provincial correctional institutions. This organization is the only one with any practical experience in this particular field. I think we would do well to listen to suggestions made by this organization with regard to treatment centres.
I have received correspondence from the director of this foundation with regard to this particular bill. The letter is dated June 6, and reads in part as follows:
Reference is made to "treatment centres", and one hopes that the federal government will soon begin the construction of such a centre in this area. 1 would draw your attention again to our belief that such an institution should be located close to the metropolitan area, the further away it is the less effective will be the personal communication and the liaison necessary between the institutional and community (rehabilitation and after care) services, and it is the latter, or rather the absence of the latter, that has been the major cause of breakdown in various treatment programs.
The point with which I am concerned is the fact that the treatment centre is going to be established at Matsqui, about 50 miles away from the centre of Vancouver. Second, we already have this narcotic addiction foundation, and I am still not too clear despite the discussion last day, as to how it fits into the over-all picture. I do not believe it fits in with clause 19 of this bill because then it would be considered as part of a penitentiary. In view of the objectives of the foundation, which I should like to put on the record, I think it is important to consider how the foundation is going to be placed in the picture.
In the fifth annual report, 1960-61, the president, Mr. Carson, set out the following objectives:
As a community-based agency, the narcotic addiction foundation is in an ideal position to carry out the following functions:
(a)	to accept, on a voluntary basis, any or all narcotic addicts who request help;
(b)	to work closely with the probation officers (as and when appointed) in the after care and rehabilitation program of the federal government;
(c)	to act as a training centre for physicians, social workers, nurses, volunteer groups, and others directly concerned with the rehabilitation of the addict in the community;
Narcotic Drug Control
(d)	to carry out an educational program among such groups as: professional personnel; educators; adolescent and young people's groups; employers; service clubs, etc;
(e)	to initiate or co-ordinate research into such aspects of drug addiction as the following:
(i)	the natural history of addiction;
(ii)	the epidemiology of addiction;
(iii)	the effectiveness of a voluntary treatment program, and if there is a group of addicts that will respond to such an attempt;
(iv)	the relationship between narcotic and other forms of addiction;
(v)	the relationship between narcotic addiction and other social disorders.
To achieve these objectives, the foundation will need to become an increasingly independent organization, feeling free to draw on financial and other resources from all levels of government and from the community we are attempting to serve.
The point that interests me particularly is how this foundation is going to fit into the new legislation. Second, this is the only organization of which I know with any direct experience in the treatment of addicts, and it is located in downtown Vancouver. It is a voluntary treatment centre which is treating, I think, approximately eight in-patients as well as a considerable number of out-patients.
